import { retestCase } from "./retest-author";

export const architectRetest = [
  ...retestCase("Customer Support Resolution Agent", "You are building a customer support resolution agent with the Claude Agent SDK. It handles returns, billing disputes and account issues through custom MCP tools: get_customer, lookup_order, process_refund and escalate_to_human. A coordinator delegates independent checks to subagents. Refunds above $500 need a supervisor's approval recorded against the refund case revision. The target is 80% first-contact resolution with correct escalation.", [
    ["arch-coast-result", "1.1", "stop", "One response asks for get_customer and lookup_order together, with stop_reason \"tool_use\". get_customer returns a profile, but lookup_order fails with an upstream 504 after 30 seconds. Logs show the next request sometimes leaves out lookup_order's result, and Claude then tells customers that no orders exist. Which next request keeps the agent loop correct?",
      "Append the assistant turn unchanged, then one user message with both tool_result blocks, lookup_order's marked is_error: true.",
      "Every tool_use block needs a matching tool_result, sent together in the next user message after the unchanged assistant turn. A failed call is still answered: set is_error and describe the failure so Claude can retry, change course or explain. Empty success hides outages; split or dropped results break the exchange.",
      "Append the assistant turn, then return lookup_order's tool_result as an empty JSON array so the loop continues without an error.",
      "Send get_customer's tool_result now and hold lookup_order's result for a later user message once a retry of it succeeds.",
      "Start a new request without tools, pasting the customer profile and the 504 message as user text so Claude can explain.",
      ["Both results go back in one user message with matching tool_use_id values, and is_error tells Claude the 504 was an outage rather than an empty order history.",
        "An empty array is a valid 'no orders' answer, which is exactly the false statement customers are receiving. Hiding the 504 removes Claude's chance to retry or explain.",
        "Results for one assistant turn must all arrive in the next user message. Leaving lookup_order's tool_use unanswered makes the request invalid and stalls the loop.",
        "Dropping the tool exchange orphans the original tool_use blocks and discards the loop's structure, so Claude cannot retry lookup_order or continue the investigation."]],
    ["arch-coast-terminal", "1.1", "stop", "Refund summaries for the supervisor queue are generated with max_tokens set to 800. Traces show 6% of responses end with stop_reason \"max_tokens\"; the final line stops mid-sentence, and about half of those lack the refund_amount field. Supervisors still approve every refund separately. How should the agent loop treat these responses?",
      "Treat max_tokens as incomplete: continue or regenerate with a higher limit, then validate the full summary before it is queued.",
      "stop_reason states why generation ended. Only end_turn means Claude finished; max_tokens means output was cut and must be continued or regenerated, then validated, before anything consumes it. Downstream approvals judge business decisions, not completeness, and shorter-output prompts only reduce how often truncation happens.",
      "Queue summaries whose parseable fields pass validation, because the supervisor's approval step will catch any detail that is missing.",
      "Treat max_tokens like end_turn because Claude stopped generating, and record the truncation rate for the weekly quality review.",
      "Add a system-prompt instruction asking Claude to keep each summary under 600 words so it finishes before the limit.",
      ["This recognizes the cut-off output as unfinished and checks the complete summary, including refund_amount, before a supervisor sees it.",
        "Supervisors approve the refund decision; they are not a completeness check. Half of the truncated summaries already lack refund_amount, which parseable fragments do not reveal.",
        "max_tokens and end_turn mean different things. Treating them alike passes truncated text downstream, and a weekly review only measures the damage afterwards.",
        "A length instruction lowers how often the limit is hit but cannot guarantee it, so the loop still needs a rule for the 6% that end on max_tokens."]],
    ["arch-coast-parallel", "1.2", "subagents", "A billing dispute needs two independent checks: whether charge ch_2 settled in the payments system and whether order 8812 shipped. In testing, a payments subagent and an orders subagent ran one after the other, and each also queried the other's system. Handling time rose from 40 to 95 seconds, and the two returned different refund recommendations. Which orchestration is most effective?",
      "Give each subagent one system, run both in parallel, and let the coordinator reconcile the findings against policy.",
      "In hub-and-spoke orchestration the coordinator partitions scope so each subagent owns a distinct slice, runs independent slices in parallel, and keeps the reconciliation decision itself. Overlapping tool access duplicates work and produces competing conclusions, while peer-to-peer handoffs remove the coordinator's control over error handling and policy.",
      "Give both subagents the payments and orders tools so either one can complete the whole check if the other fails.",
      "Let the two subagents exchange findings directly, then have the orders subagent submit the final refund recommendation.",
      "Merge both checks into one subagent with every billing tool so a single context produces one consistent recommendation.",
      ["Distinct scopes stop the duplicate queries, parallel execution recovers the 55 seconds, and one reconciliation point resolves the conflicting recommendations.",
        "Overlapping access is what caused both subagents to query both systems. It keeps the duplicated work and still yields two competing recommendations.",
        "Subagent-to-subagent traffic bypasses the coordinator, which loses observability and lets one specialist make the policy decision the coordinator should own.",
        "One broad subagent avoids conflicting outputs but gives up parallelism and concentrates unrelated tools in a single context, so handling time stays high."]],
    ["arch-coast-coordinator", "1.2", "agents", "For a $620 damaged-item claim, the policy subagent reports a $750 auto-approval limit, and the account subagent reports $500. Trace metadata shows the $750 figure came from refund-policy v3, retired on 1 September, and the $500 figure from v4. The coordinator currently adopts whichever finding includes more supporting detail. What should the coordinator do instead?",
      "Check each finding's policy version and effective date, apply v4's $500 limit, and note why the v3 figure was set aside.",
      "When subagents disagree, the coordinator needs a resolution rule based on provenance: which source is current, authoritative and applicable. Self-rated confidence, detail and forced consensus do not establish correctness. Escalate when the evidence cannot settle a conflict, not when metadata already shows one value comes from a retired source.",
      "Ask both subagents to rate their confidence from 1 to 10 and adopt the limit from the more confident subagent.",
      "Send each subagent the other's finding and rerun both until they converge on a single limit for this claim.",
      "Escalate the claim to a supervisor because the subagents disagree, even though one of the values comes from a retired policy.",
      ["The metadata already settles the conflict: v3 is retired. Applying v4 and recording the rejection keeps the decision explainable.",
        "Self-reported confidence is poorly calibrated and says nothing about which policy version is in force on the claim date.",
        "Agreement reached by exchanging answers is not evidence. Both subagents could settle on the retired $750 limit.",
        "Escalation is for conflicts the evidence cannot resolve. Here the trace shows which source is current, so escalating adds cost without improving the outcome."]],
    ["arch-coast-context", "1.3", "subagents", "The coordinator spawns a subagent to decide whether order 8812 qualifies for a damage replacement. Earlier in its own conversation the coordinator verified customer C-20931 and collected two damage photos. On its first run the subagent made 4 tool calls and returned 'need customer details', because it could not see that conversation. Which TWO items belong in the subagent's prompt?",
      ["The verified customer ID C-20931, order 8812, and references to both damage photos, copied into the prompt.", "The expected return format: eligibility decision, the policy clause applied, and any evidence that is missing or unclear."],
      "A non-fork subagent starts with a fresh context: its definition plus the prompt string it receives. The coordinator must pass verified facts and evidence explicitly and state the output contract. Pointers to history the subagent never saw fail, and extra tools or bulk transcripts widen authority and noise without helping the task.",
      "An instruction to read the coordinator's earlier conversation, which the subagent can reach through the shared session.",
      "The process_refund tool, so the subagent can issue the replacement refund as soon as it confirms eligibility.",
      "Every customer transcript from today, so the subagent has as much context as possible for its judgment.",
      ["The subagent failed because it lacked these facts. Passing the verified ID, order and photo references removes the 4 wasted calls.",
        "An explicit output contract lets the coordinator check the decision and see which evidence the subagent could not confirm.",
        "Subagents do not inherit the coordinator's conversation, which is exactly why the first run asked for customer details.",
        "Eligibility checking does not need refund authority. Adding process_refund widens the subagent's permissions beyond its role.",
        "Bulk transcripts add unrelated customers' data and noise. The subagent needs this case's verified facts, not everything."]],
    ["arch-coast-owned", "1.3", "subagents", "The coordinator asks a billing subagent whether charge ch_2 on 2 November duplicated ch_1. Last week, 3 of 40 returns said 'duplicate confirmed' without naming any charge, and supervisors spent about 6 minutes each re-tracing them in the ledger. Which return format should the subagent's prompt require?",
      "A structured finding with the verdict, both charge IDs, their amounts and timestamps, and the ledger query used.",
      "Subagent outputs should be compact, structured and verifiable: a claim plus the identifiers and evidence that support it. Then the coordinator and people can check findings without redoing the work. Narratives drop provenance, raw dumps shift the work to reviewers, and self-reported confidence does not substitute for the evidence.",
      "A short customer-facing explanation of the duplicate, with a reassuring note about when the refund will arrive.",
      "The verdict plus a confidence score from 1 to 10, so supervisors review only the low-confidence duplicate findings.",
      "A full export of the customer's monthly ledger, so supervisors can confirm the duplicate charge themselves if needed.",
      ["Charge IDs, amounts, timestamps and the query make each verdict checkable in seconds instead of the 6 minutes spent re-tracing.",
        "Customer wording serves a different audience and omits the identifiers the coordinator needs to verify the duplicate claim.",
        "A high self-rated score could accompany the same unsupported 'duplicate confirmed' answers, which would then skip review entirely.",
        "A raw ledger moves the tracing work back to supervisors, which is the cost the format change is meant to remove."]],
    ["arch-coast-approve", "1.4", "agents", "Refunds above $500 need a supervisor's approval. The system prompt states the rule, yet an audit of 2,000 sessions found 9 refunds between $520 and $940 that process_refund executed without one. In each case the customer insisted a supervisor had already agreed. Which change most effectively prevents a recurrence?",
      "Gate process_refund in code so amounts above $500 are refused unless an approval record exists for that refund.",
      "When a prerequisite has financial consequences, enforce it at the tool boundary in code. Prompt rules and examples lower the miss rate but cannot reach zero, especially under customer pressure. Post-hoc reports detect violations after money has moved; a gate on the call itself makes the unapproved refund impossible.",
      "Strengthen the system prompt with a bolded rule and an example refusing refunds above $500 without approval.",
      "Require the agent to confirm in its reply that a supervisor approved before it calls process_refund over $500.",
      "Flag refunds above $500 in a nightly report so supervisors can reverse any that lacked approval on the next business day.",
      ["A deterministic gate checks for a real approval record, so a customer's claim cannot unlock the refund however persuasive it is.",
        "The rule already exists in the prompt and failed 9 times. Stronger wording and one example remain probabilistic compliance.",
        "The agent's own statement is what the customers manipulated. Self-attestation is not evidence that a supervisor approved anything.",
        "A nightly report is a detective control. The money has already moved, and reversals cost more than preventing the call."]],
    ["arch-coast-handoff", "1.4", "agents", "A supervisor approved refund case R-311 revision 3 for $640. The customer then reported a second damaged item, and the agent updated the case to revision 4 for $910. The approval check looks up approvals by case ID only, so an automatic retry of process_refund paid $910. Which gate would have caught this?",
      "Require an approval record whose case ID, revision and amount all match the refund being executed.",
      "An approval authorizes specific content, not an identifier. Bind it to the exact revision and amount, and check that binding where the external action happens so retries and edits re-trigger the prerequisite. Time windows, caller identity, audit logs and evidence checks each answer a different question.",
      "Accept a supervisor approval for case R-311 while it is less than 24 hours old, as the current check does.",
      "Verify that the caller is a current supervisor and record the outgoing amount in the refund audit log.",
      "Reuse the latest approval when the new revision has passed the damage-photo verification step before the retry.",
      ["Matching revision and amount at execution time would have refused the $910 payment, because only revision 3 for $640 was approved.",
        "A recent approval for the case still covers only revision 3. A time window does not notice that the content changed.",
        "Checking the caller and logging the amount do not establish that this $910 refund was ever approved.",
        "Photo verification establishes evidence for the new item, but the higher amount still needs the supervisor's decision."]],
    ["arch-coast-hook", "1.5", "hooks", "The team wants process_refund calls above $500 blocked, with Claude steered to escalate_to_human. A draft Agent SDK configuration registers a callback under PostToolUse with matcher mcp__support__process_refund that returns a denial message. In testing, the ledger recorded the refund before the message appeared. Which configuration fixes this?",
      "Register the callback under PreToolUse and return permissionDecision \"deny\" with a permissionDecisionReason pointing to escalate_to_human.",
      "PreToolUse runs after Claude requests a tool and before it executes, so it can deny or rewrite the call. PostToolUse runs after the side effect and can only change what Claude sees. Limits with financial consequences belong in a PreToolUse hook whose denial reason steers Claude to the approved alternative.",
      "Keep PostToolUse but return updatedToolOutput that replaces the ledger response with an error, so Claude escalates instead.",
      "Register a SessionStart callback that loads the $500 refund limit into context, and let Claude decide when it applies.",
      "Set refund_limit: 500 in .claude/settings.json so the Agent SDK rejects process_refund calls above that amount on its own.",
      ["PreToolUse fires before process_refund runs, so the deny decision stops the payment and the reason tells Claude to escalate.",
        "updatedToolOutput changes what Claude reads, but the refund has already posted. PostToolUse cannot undo a side effect.",
        "Loading the limit into context leaves enforcement to Claude's judgment, which is the probabilistic control the team is replacing.",
        "There is no refund_limit setting. Business limits are enforced by hook code or the tool itself, not by an invented settings key."]],
    ["arch-coast-normalize", "1.5", "hooks", "Order IDs reach process_refund as 8812, #8812 and ORD-8812. The approval lookup expects ORD-8812, so 14% of legitimate refunds were wrongly denied last week. One engineer proposes fuzzy matching, but a test showed it matched order 88120 to approval 8812. Which approach normalizes IDs while keeping the approval check reliable?",
      "A PreToolUse hook rewrites the ID to canonical form with updatedInput, and the approval check then uses that value.",
      "Normalize inputs deterministically before the check that depends on them. A PreToolUse hook with updatedInput gives the approval logic and the tool one canonical value. Prompt formatting stays probabilistic, post-execution rewrites come too late, and fuzzy matching trades false denials for approvals attached to the wrong record.",
      "Add a system-prompt instruction and three examples telling Claude to format order IDs as ORD- followed by digits.",
      "A PostToolUse hook rewrites the order ID in the tool response after process_refund has checked approval on the raw ID.",
      "Have the approval service accept a fuzzy match on the numeric part of any order ID it receives.",
      ["One deterministic canonical form, produced before execution, removes the false denials without loosening which approval matches which order.",
        "Examples raise consistency but cannot guarantee it, so some refunds would still reach the lookup in the wrong format.",
        "The approval check has already run on the raw ID, so rewriting the response afterwards cannot prevent the false denial.",
        "The test already showed fuzzy matching attaching approval 8812 to order 88120, which would release money on the wrong order."]],
    ["arch-coast-split", "1.6", "agents", "A ticket reads: 'Order 8812 arrived damaged, I was charged twice in November, and please change my email.' The current fixed chain handles one concern at a time and drafts a reply after each. Customers received 3 separate replies, and the second reply's refund ignored the first's. Which decomposition is most effective?",
      "Split it into three concerns, check them in parallel for the same customer, then send one combined reply.",
      "Decompose multi-concern requests into distinct items, run independent investigations in parallel against shared verified context, and synthesize once all results are in. Sequential handling wastes time when items do not depend on each other, and drafting before the evidence arrives produces replies that contradict or must be retracted.",
      "Resolve the first concern fully, reply to the customer, and ask them to open new tickets for the other two issues.",
      "Draft the reply from the damage check first, then revise the same draft as the billing and email results arrive later.",
      "Run the three concerns sequentially in one shared context so each later check can see the earlier tool results.",
      ["Parallel checks against one verified customer, followed by a single synthesis, fix both the triple replies and the conflicting refunds.",
        "Pushing two concerns back to the customer lowers first-contact resolution and still leaves related refunds uncoordinated.",
        "Drafting before the billing result arrives repeats the current problem: early replies that later evidence contradicts.",
        "The concerns are independent, so serial handling adds latency without improving accuracy, and it still needs one final synthesis."]],
    ["arch-coast-replan", "1.6", "agents", "During an audit of 120 disputed charges, the payments API starts returning 503 with an estimated 60-minute outage. The orders subagent has already verified 74 shipments, and 46 charge checks are pending. The current pipeline restarts the whole audit whenever any step fails. What should the coordinator do?",
      "Keep the 74 verified shipment results, mark the 46 charge checks blocked by the 503, and replan them for retry.",
      "Explicit dependencies let a coordinator keep completed work and replan only the blocked branch. Record what failed and why so the gap stays visible, then retry under policy. Restarting discards valid results, tight retry loops waste capacity during a known outage, and treating unchecked items as clean hides the coverage gap.",
      "Restart the full audit once the outage ends so every shipment and charge check reflects the same point in time.",
      "Report the audit complete using the shipment results, and record the 46 unchecked charges as not disputed.",
      "Retry the 46 charge checks every few seconds until the payments API responds again, holding all other work meanwhile.",
      ["The verified shipments stay valid, the blocked checks are explicit, and the retry can be scheduled around the stated outage.",
        "Shipment data does not depend on the payments outage, so restarting throws away 74 valid results for no gain.",
        "Recording unchecked charges as not disputed turns a known gap into a false finding in the audit report.",
        "A tight retry loop during a declared 60-minute outage wastes calls and blocks unrelated work without improving the outcome."]],
    ["arch-coast-resume", "1.7", "sessions", "Yesterday a session named dispute-8812 gathered ledger reads, two policy lookups and a draft refund plan. Overnight the bank posted a reversal, so only the ledger entry for charge ch_2 changed; the policies and order data are unchanged. Which way of continuing today is most effective?",
      "Resume dispute-8812 and tell it the ch_2 ledger entry changed, so it re-reads that entry and rechecks the plan.",
      "Resume when most of a session's tool results still hold, and name exactly what changed so Claude re-reads those sources instead of trusting stale observations. Start fresh with a summary when most context is stale, and fork when you want two directions from one baseline, not to settle a factual question.",
      "Start a fresh session with no summary and let the agent rerun every ledger, policy and order lookup from scratch.",
      "Resume dispute-8812 without mentioning the reversal, since the agent will notice it when it next reads the ledger.",
      "Fork dispute-8812 into one branch that assumes the reversal applies and one that does not, then compare their plans.",
      ["Most context is still valid, and naming the one changed entry targets re-analysis where the stale read would mislead the plan.",
        "Discarding a mostly valid session repeats work on unchanged policies and orders and loses yesterday's reasoning.",
        "A resumed session trusts its earlier tool results and may never re-read the ledger, so the stale ch_2 entry drives the plan.",
        "The reversal is a fact to check, not a design choice. Two speculative branches add work without resolving which is true."]],
    ["arch-coast-branch", "1.7", "sessions", "A 40-minute Agent SDK session, sess_7f2, analyzed 300 refund-policy edge cases. The team now wants two alternative escalation designs, one threshold-based and one category-based, each built on that same analysis without either design seeing the other's work. Which call pattern achieves this?",
      "Run two queries with resume set to sess_7f2 and fork_session=True, so each gets a new session ID from the shared history.",
      "Forking copies a session's history into a new session ID and leaves the original unchanged, so two alternatives start from one baseline. In the Agent SDK pass resume with fork_session=True (forkSession in TypeScript); in the CLI use --resume with --fork-session. Plain resume or continue appends to one thread.",
      "Run two queries with resume set to sess_7f2 and no fork option, so each design continues the original session.",
      "Call session.clone() on sess_7f2 twice and pass each clone's ID as the session option of a new query() call.",
      "Start two queries with continue_conversation=True so each one picks up the most recent analysis session in this directory.",
      ["Each fork receives a copy of the 40-minute analysis under its own session ID, so neither design sees the other's turns.",
        "Without fork_session both queries append to sess_7f2, so the second design would see the first design's work in its history.",
        "The Agent SDK has no session.clone() method. Branching a session is done by passing resume together with fork_session.",
        "continue_conversation resumes the most recent conversation, so the second query would continue the first design rather than the analysis."]],
    ["arch-coast-brief", "1.3", "subagents", "A coordinator has AgentDefinition entries for billing-checker and order-checker but never delegates. Logs show it calling lookup_order itself, and in one headless run a permission prompt stalled the job for 10 minutes. Its options set allowed_tools=[\"Read\", \"mcp__support__lookup_order\"]. What is the most likely root cause?",
      "allowed_tools omits the Task tool (Agent in newer docs), so the coordinator cannot spawn subagents without a permission prompt.",
      "A coordinator spawns subagents through the Task tool, which current docs call Agent. It must appear in allowedTools or allowed_tools, or each spawn waits on permission. A stalled prompt plus direct tool use points to that list. Description quality affects which subagent is chosen, not whether spawning is allowed.",
      "The billing-checker and order-checker descriptions are too short, so the coordinator never matches its task to either subagent.",
      "The options lack subagents_enabled=True, which the SDK requires before AgentDefinition entries become available to the coordinator.",
      "Each AgentDefinition must repeat the coordinator's allowed tools, so the subagents inherit permission to call lookup_order.",
      ["The permission prompt and the coordinator doing the lookup itself both indicate that spawning was not pre-approved in allowed_tools.",
        "Vague descriptions can cause the wrong subagent to be chosen, but they do not explain a stalled permission prompt in a headless run.",
        "There is no subagents_enabled option. Defining agents and allowing the Task or Agent tool is what makes delegation possible.",
        "Subagent tool lists are set per AgentDefinition and do not need to mirror the coordinator. That would not unblock the spawn."]],
    ["arch-coast-return", "1.4", "agents", "A supervisor rejects escalated refund R-418 revision 2 for $730, noting that the second damage photo shows a different order number. The agent's state machine returns the case to drafting but keeps the approval flag from an earlier revision 1 approval of $410. Which state transition is correct?",
      "Send the case back to photo checking, void the revision 1 sign-off, and require new sign-off for the next revision.",
      "A rejection changes workflow state and invalidates approvals that do not match the content that will finally execute. Send the case back to the step that resolves the identified problem, and require a fresh approval bound to the resulting revision. Reusing stale approvals or closing the case both mishandle a correctable evidence issue.",
      "Return the case to drafting and keep the revision 1 approval, since $410 is below the amount the customer now requests.",
      "Remove the second photo, reduce the refund to $410, and process it under the existing revision 1 approval.",
      "Close the case as rejected, notify the customer, and require a brand-new ticket before further investigation can begin.",
      ["The rejection concerns evidence, so the case returns to evidence review, and any later refund needs approval of the content actually paid.",
        "A leftover flag from revision 1 would let a retry pay without approval of whatever revision 3 eventually contains.",
        "Changing the claim unilaterally and reusing an old approval skips both the evidence question and the supervisor's decision.",
        "The problem is a correctable photo mismatch. Closing the case and forcing a new ticket hurts first-contact resolution unnecessarily."]],
  ]),
  ...retestCase("Developer Productivity", "You are building developer productivity tools with the Claude Agent SDK. The agent helps engineers explore an unfamiliar inventory platform, understand legacy modules and automate repetitive tasks. It uses the built-in Read, Write, Bash, Grep and Glob tools and connects to MCP servers, including the team's inventory server, which has read and write tools, and a shared documentation server. Several teams share the repository.", [
    ["arch-museum-boundary", "2.1", "tool", "Logs from the inventory MCP server show that for 11% of requests such as 'check the stock record for SKU 4471', Claude calls update_item instead of get_item. Their descriptions read 'Handles item records' and 'Works with items', and both accept a sku parameter. What is the most effective first step?",
      "Rewrite both descriptions to state what each tool does, its side effects, its inputs, and when to use the other one.",
      "Tool names and descriptions are Claude's main selection signal. When two tools have vague, overlapping descriptions, the proportionate first fix is to describe each one's purpose, side effects, inputs and boundary with its neighbor. Examples, routers and consolidation add cost or complexity before the definitions themselves are fixed.",
      "Add eight few-shot examples to the system prompt showing stock questions routed to get_item rather than update_item.",
      "Add a keyword router that inspects each request and exposes only get_item when the request contains the word 'check'.",
      "Merge both tools into one item tool with an action parameter that the server validates before reading or writing.",
      ["The descriptions give Claude nothing to separate a read from a write. Stating purpose, side effects and boundaries fixes the root cause cheaply.",
        "Examples add tokens and help somewhat, but they leave both definitions vague, so new phrasings will keep misrouting.",
        "A keyword router is brittle and bypasses Claude's language understanding; requests like 'look up' or 'what's the stock' would slip past it.",
        "Consolidation can be a valid design, but it is more work than a first step warrants when the immediate problem is thin descriptions."]],
    ["arch-museum-filter", "2.1", "tool", "The search_inspections tool takes one string parameter, query. Traces show Claude sending values like 'SKU 4471 last spring' and '4471 2025-04', and 23% of calls return the wrong inspection because the server guesses which part is the date. How should the tool's input contract change?",
      "Define separate sku and inspected_on fields in input_schema, with formats, examples and required fields marked.",
      "Typed, named parameters with formats and examples make Claude's intent explicit and let the server validate inputs before running. A free-form string forces guessing on one side or the other. Description notes alone stay probabilistic, server-side guessing hides ambiguity, and raw query access widens risk far beyond the need.",
      "Keep the single query string and document in the description that the date must follow the SKU in ISO format.",
      "Have the server retry each failed search with other date interpretations and return the closest match it finds.",
      "Expose a raw SQL parameter so Claude can write precise WHERE clauses against the inspections table directly.",
      ["Separate typed fields remove the parsing ambiguity behind the 23% wrong results and let the server reject malformed dates.",
        "A format note helps, but the server still parses one free-form string, so any deviation reproduces the wrong-date results.",
        "Returning the closest guess converts ambiguity into confidently wrong inspections, which is the failure being reported.",
        "Raw SQL gives the agent far more power than a lookup needs and moves validation onto every generated query."]],
    ["arch-museum-missing", "2.2", "tool", "When an item has been archived, get_item returns {\"items\": []} with no error. In 17 of 50 sampled sessions, Claude then told engineers the SKU never existed and suggested creating a duplicate record. Archived items can still be read through get_archived_item. What should get_item return instead?",
      "A structured result saying the item is archived, with its archive date and a pointer to get_archived_item.",
      "Tool results should say what actually happened in a form Claude can act on. An archived record is a business outcome, not a failure and not an absence, so return its status and the permitted next step. Generic errors invite useless retries, disguised data misleads, and prompt notes cannot disambiguate an empty list.",
      "An isError result with a generic 'lookup failed' message so Claude retries the same request before answering.",
      "The archived record's fields presented as an active item, so engineers still see the data they asked for.",
      "The same empty list, plus a system-prompt note explaining that empty results may mean the item was archived.",
      ["Stating the archived status and the next tool stops the 'never existed' answers and the duplicate records they caused.",
        "Retrying will return the same outcome, and a generic message still hides that the item exists in the archive.",
        "Presenting archived data as active misleads engineers about the item's real state and invites edits to retired records.",
        "An empty list also means a genuine miss, so a prompt note cannot tell Claude which case applies to this SKU."]],
    ["arch-museum-retry", "2.2", "errors", "The documentation server's search_docs fails in two ways. About 4% of calls time out under load and succeed on retry, while calls with an unsupported version value such as '2.x' fail every time. Both return isError: true with the text 'search failed', and Claude retries each up to 5 times. What should the error result contain?",
      "An error category and a retryable flag, so timeouts get retried and bad versions return the accepted values.",
      "Structured errors should tell the agent whether a retry can help. Transient faults like timeouts are retryable; validation faults need corrected input, so return the category and the accepted values. Generic messages cause pointless retries, longer timeouts only reduce frequency, and empty success hides a real input problem from Claude.",
      "One retryable status for both cases, with the precise cause written only to the server's diagnostic log.",
      "A longer server timeout of 60 seconds so fewer calls fail, keeping the existing generic error text unchanged.",
      "Empty successful results for invalid versions so Claude stops retrying, while timeouts keep the error flag.",
      ["Claude can retry the 4% of timeouts and immediately correct the version instead of wasting 5 attempts on a call that cannot succeed.",
        "The log helps operators, but Claude still sees an identical retryable error and keeps retrying '2.x' five times.",
        "A longer timeout reduces how often searches fail; it does nothing for invalid versions, which fail regardless of time.",
        "Empty success tells Claude the docs contain nothing for that version, hiding that the input itself was wrong."]],
    ["arch-museum-read", "2.3", "safety", "A documentation subagent summarizes inspection history for engineers. It currently receives all 14 inventory tools, including update_item and delete_item, and last week it called update_item twice to fix typos it noticed. Its role needs read access plus an occasional single-field check against the source. Which tool configuration is most appropriate?",
      "Limit the subagent to get_item and search_inspections, plus a narrow verify_field tool for the occasional check.",
      "Scope each agent's tools to its role, adding a constrained cross-role tool only for a frequent narrow need. Prompt rules and logs leave write capability in place, while stripping every tool forces costly manual context passing. Least privilege reduces both misuse and the number of options Claude must weigh on each turn.",
      "Keep all 14 tools and add a rule to the subagent's prompt forbidding writes unless an engineer explicitly asks.",
      "Keep all 14 tools but log each update_item call by the subagent for a weekly review by the record owners.",
      "Remove the inventory tools and have the coordinator paste the records into the subagent's prompt for each request.",
      ["Read tools cover the summaries, the narrow verifier covers the occasional check, and write tools are no longer available to misuse.",
        "A prompt rule is probabilistic, and the subagent has already shown it will write when it decides a fix is helpful.",
        "Logging detects edits after records have changed. The unwanted capability remains, and owners must clean up later.",
        "Removing every tool over-corrects: the coordinator must now fetch and pass records the subagent could safely read itself."]],
    ["arch-museum-toolcount", "2.3", "tool", "A scaffolding step must return a structured file plan by calling one of three tools: plan_service, plan_library or plan_script. In 9% of runs Claude instead answers in prose ('Here's how I'd structure it...'), and the next step crashes. Requests currently send tool_choice {\"type\": \"auto\"}. Which change most directly fixes this?",
      "Set tool_choice to {\"type\": \"any\"} so Claude must call one of the three planning tools and still chooses which.",
      "tool_choice controls whether Claude must call a tool. auto lets it answer in text; any requires a call to one of the provided tools while Claude picks which; a named tool forces one specific tool; none blocks tools. When several schemas could apply and prose is unacceptable, use any rather than prompt rules.",
      "Set tool_choice to {\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"plan_service\"} so every request returns a structured plan.",
      "Set tool_choice to {\"type\": \"required\"} so Claude cannot answer in text while planning tools are available.",
      "Keep auto and add a system-prompt rule saying Claude must call a planning tool before it writes explanatory text.",
      ["any guarantees a tool call, which removes the prose replies, while leaving Claude free to pick the plan type that fits the project.",
        "Forcing plan_service guarantees a tool call but sends library and script projects through the wrong planning schema.",
        "The Claude API's tool_choice types are auto, any, tool and none. 'required' does not exist and would be rejected.",
        "A prompt rule lowers the 9% failure rate but cannot guarantee a tool call the way tool_choice can."]],
    ["arch-museum-config", "2.4", "mcpConfig", "Six engineers each configured the team inventory MCP server by hand, and two copies still point at a retired URL. One engineer proposes committing a shared configuration that includes the server's API token. Which TWO steps make the setup reproducible for the team without sharing secrets?",
      ["Commit the server to the repository's .mcp.json, added with claude mcp add --scope project, so every clone gets it.", "Reference the token as ${INVENTORY_TOKEN} in .mcp.json so each engineer and CI runner supplies it from the environment."],
      "Team-wide MCP servers belong in the committed project .mcp.json, which --scope project writes, so every clone gets the same definition. Secrets stay out of version control through ${VAR} expansion, letting each developer and runner provide credentials. Per-user copies drift, literal tokens leak into git history, and there is no team scope key.",
      "Commit .mcp.json with the literal API token included, since the repository is private to the team anyway.",
      "Copy one engineer's working server entry from ~/.claude.json into each other engineer's home configuration.",
      "Add a \"scope\": \"team\" key to each engineer's user configuration so Claude Code syncs the server for everyone.",
      ["A single committed project definition replaces six hand-made copies, so the retired URL cannot persist on some machines.",
        "Environment variable expansion keeps the token out of the repository while every machine still connects with its own credential.",
        "A literal token in git reaches everyone with repository access and stays in history after it is rotated.",
        "Copied personal entries are exactly how the retired URLs survived. Each copy drifts independently of the repository.",
        "MCP scopes are local, project and user; there is no team key, and user configuration is never synced to others."]],
    ["arch-museum-precedence", "2.4", "mcpConfig", "The repository's .mcp.json defines an inventory server at the team endpoint, and CI reaches it correctly. On one engineer's laptop, Claude Code reaches a sandbox URL for inventory instead. The commit, model and project file are identical, and claude mcp list shows an inventory entry in local scope. What is the root cause?",
      "A same-named local-scope entry in ~/.claude.json takes precedence over the project entry, and the two are not merged.",
      "Claude Code reads MCP servers from local, project and user scopes. When the same name appears in several, local beats project, which beats user, and entries are not merged. Inspect the scopes with claude mcp list before editing shared configuration, since the project file is already correct for everyone else.",
      "User-scope entries override project entries, so the engineer's user configuration must contain the sandbox URL.",
      "Claude Code merges same-named entries field by field, so the laptop combined the project command with a local URL.",
      "The laptop lacks a .claude/mcp.lock file that pins resolved endpoints, so Claude Code picked the first match it found.",
      ["The listing shows a local-scope inventory entry, and local scope wins over the committed project definition on that machine.",
        "Precedence runs local, then project, then user. A user entry would lose to the project file, so it cannot explain this.",
        "Same-named entries are not merged; the winning scope's definition is used whole, URL and command together.",
        "Claude Code has no MCP lock file. Resolution follows scope precedence, which the claude mcp list output already explains."]],
    ["arch-museum-local", "2.5", "cli", "An engineer asks what the reorder_threshold field means and pastes the path docs/inventory/fields.md, where it is defined. In last week's traces, similar questions took the agent 9 tool calls and about 40,000 tokens because it searched the whole repository first. Which approach is most efficient here?",
      "Read docs/inventory/fields.md directly, then answer from the field's definition and cite the section it came from.",
      "Match the tool to what you already know. When the path is known, Read it directly; Grep finds text whose location is unknown, and Glob finds files by name. Searching or bulk-reading when the evidence location is already given spends calls and context without improving the answer.",
      "Grep the whole repository for reorder_threshold first to confirm the pasted file is the authoritative definition.",
      "Glob for **/*.md under docs/ and Read each matching file so the answer covers related documentation too.",
      "Call the documentation server's search tool for reorder_threshold, then Read whichever result pages it ranks highest.",
      ["One Read of the known file answers the question with a citation, instead of 9 calls and 40,000 tokens of searching.",
        "A repository-wide Grep repeats the expensive pattern from last week when the engineer has already supplied the location.",
        "Reading every Markdown file under docs/ fills context with unrelated pages to answer a single-field question.",
        "An external search adds a dependency and may rank other pages first, while the authoritative file is already known."]],
    ["arch-museum-search", "2.5", "cli", "Before changing its signature, an engineer needs every caller of calculateReorderPoint. The repository has about 3,200 files, and lib/index.ts re-exports the function as reorderPoint. A first attempt found 6 callers, but the build then failed in 4 files that import the alias. Which exploration sequence gives complete, focused evidence?",
      "Grep for calculateReorderPoint and reorderPoint, Read the definition and re-export, then Read each caller found.",
      "Explore incrementally: Grep for content to find definitions and callers, then Read only what the trace requires. When a wrapper re-exports a function under another name, search for each exported name, or callers that use the alias are missed. Glob finds file names, not code, and reading everything up front wastes context.",
      "Glob for files with names like *reorder*, Read each match, and treat those files as the complete set of callers.",
      "Read lib/index.ts and each file it imports, so the agent builds a full dependency map before searching for callers.",
      "Grep for calculateReorderPoint again with a broader glob filter, then update the definition and each matched file.",
      ["Searching both exported names catches the 4 alias callers the first attempt missed, and targeted Reads keep context small.",
        "Glob matches paths, not code. Callers in files without 'reorder' in their names would be missed entirely.",
        "Following imports from the wrapper reads many unrelated modules and still does not reveal which files call the alias.",
        "A broader file filter cannot find callers that never mention the original name, which is why the build broke."]],
    ["arch-museum-error-route", "2.2", "mcp", "Two search_inspections calls both return {\"results\": []}. One searched an existing collection and found no matches; the other failed because the engineer's token lacks access to the restricted collection. In 6 sessions, Claude told engineers that no inspections existed for restricted SKUs. Which result design lets Claude respond correctly?",
      "Return empty success when nothing is found and an isError result with a permission category when access is denied.",
      "Keep 'the search failed' distinct from 'the search succeeded and found nothing' all the way to the model. Access failures need an error result whose category explains the cause; valid empty results are findings. Flattening both into one shape, or escalating privileges to avoid the error, misleads users or bypasses authorization.",
      "Return the same empty list for both outcomes but add a total_searched count so Claude can tell them apart.",
      "Retry denied searches with a service-account token so engineers receive complete results from the restricted collection.",
      "Return isError for both outcomes so Claude treats an empty result as a failure and asks the engineer to retry.",
      ["Distinct shapes let Claude report 'none found' for one case and an access problem for the other, ending the false answers.",
        "A count is an indirect hint Claude must interpret; the shared empty shape still invites the same wrong conclusion.",
        "Escalating to a service account bypasses the access control that denied the engineer, which is not the tool's decision.",
        "Marking real no-match searches as errors produces pointless retries and hides genuine empty findings from engineers."]],
  ]),
  ...retestCase("Code Generation with Claude Code", "Your team uses Claude Code to generate, refactor, debug and document a TypeScript monorepo containing apps/web, services/search and a legacy adapters/index package that three services consume. You maintain CLAUDE.md files, custom slash commands and skills, and you decide when to use plan mode versus direct execution. A separate claude -p review job runs in CI on every pull request.", [
    ["arch-public-scopes", "3.1", "memory", "A rule stating that adapters/index owns compatibility for the v1 search API works in your sessions, but two new teammates' sessions ignore it and edit services/search directly. Running /memory on your machine shows the rule in ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, and the project's CLAUDE.md does not mention it. What is the most effective fix?",
      "Move the rule into the project's committed CLAUDE.md so every teammate's session loads it from the repository.",
      "When behavior differs between teammates, check where the instruction lives before rewording it. User-level ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md applies only to you; the committed project CLAUDE.md reaches everyone through version control. Per-person copies drift, stronger wording does not fix placement, and a personal command depends on someone remembering to run it.",
      "Ask each teammate to paste the rule into their own ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md so it matches your configuration.",
      "Reword the rule in your user CLAUDE.md with stronger emphasis so Claude applies it more reliably across sessions.",
      "Create a personal /compat command in ~/.claude/commands/ that teammates run before they edit services/search.",
      ["/memory shows the rule only at user level. Committing it to the project file gives every clone the same instruction.",
        "Per-person copies would work today but drift the moment the rule changes, and each new teammate must remember to add it.",
        "Your sessions already follow the rule. Emphasis cannot reach teammates whose sessions never load your user file.",
        "Commands in ~/.claude/commands/ exist only on your machine, and an on-demand command depends on someone choosing to run it."]],
    ["arch-public-personal", "3.1", "memory", "You want Claude Code to explain its changes as short bullet lists with file paths first, in every repository you work on. Teammates prefer prose explanations, and last sprint the team reviewed and rejected adding a style rule to the shared CLAUDE.md. Where should this preference live?",
      "In your user-level ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md, which loads in each of your projects and is not shared through git.",
      "Scope follows who should receive an instruction. Preferences that apply to you across every project belong in user-level ~/.claude/CLAUDE.md. Project files and rules reach all teammates, and CLAUDE.local.md is private but per project, so it would need a copy in each repository and would drift.",
      "In the repository's ./CLAUDE.md under a 'Personal preferences' heading marked as applying to you alone.",
      "In a gitignored ./CLAUDE.local.md in each repository, so the preference stays private but is set per project.",
      "In a .claude/rules/ file whose paths list is **/* so the style applies whenever Claude edits a file.",
      ["User-level instructions follow you into every project and never reach teammates, which matches both stated requirements.",
        "The shared file loads for the whole team, which reverses the decision the team already made about style rules.",
        "CLAUDE.local.md keeps it private, but you would maintain a copy in each repository, and new clones would lack it.",
        "Project rules are committed and load for teammates too, so a match-everything glob imposes your style on them."]],
    ["arch-public-skill", "3.2", "skills", "Before each release, about twice a month, contributors run a 7-step storage release checklist that uses 2 fixture files. You want every developer who clones or pulls the repository to get a /release-check command, and ordinary sessions should not load the checklist. Where should it be defined?",
      "As .claude/skills/release-check/SKILL.md with its fixtures in that folder, committed so git shares it with the team.",
      "Team workflows invoked on demand belong in the repository's .claude/skills/ or .claude/commands/ so version control shares them. A skill folder can bundle supporting files and loads only when used. Home-directory copies are personal and drift, CLAUDE.md loads in every session, and there is no commands array in a .claude/config.json file.",
      "As ~/.claude/skills/release-check/SKILL.md on each developer's own machine, with the two fixture files copied alongside it.",
      "In a .claude/config.json file as a commands array entry named release-check that points at the fixtures.",
      "In the root CLAUDE.md under a 'Release checklist' heading that Claude follows when someone mentions releases.",
      ["A committed project skill reaches each clone, bundles the fixtures, and loads its body only when /release-check runs.",
        "Personal skills in ~/.claude/ are not shared through the repository, so new contributors would lack the command.",
        "Claude Code has no .claude/config.json commands array. Commands and skills are Markdown files in their directories.",
        "CLAUDE.md is loaded in every session, so the checklist would occupy context during unrelated work and is not a command."]],
    ["arch-public-path", "3.3", "memory", "Every SQL migration needs a rollback note. Migration files live in services/*/db/migrations/ across 5 services, and a new service is added most quarters. You want the instruction loaded only while Claude works on those files, wherever new services put them. Which configuration does this?",
      "Create .claude/rules/migrations.md whose YAML frontmatter sets `paths: [\"services/*/db/migrations/**/*.sql\"]`, with the rollback-note rule in its body.",
      "Path-scoped rules are Markdown files in .claude/rules/ whose YAML frontmatter has a paths list of globs relative to the project root. They load only when Claude works on matching files, wherever they live. paths is the only field Claude Code reads there; directory CLAUDE.md copies need upkeep, and root sections load everywhere.",
      "Add a CLAUDE.md inside each service's db/migrations folder containing the rule, and add another for each new service.",
      "Create .claude/rules/migrations.md whose frontmatter sets `applies_to: \"**/migrations/**\"`, with the rollback-note rule in its body.",
      "Add a 'Migrations' section to the root CLAUDE.md and rely on Claude to apply it when it edits SQL files.",
      ["The paths glob matches migrations in current and future services and keeps the rule out of context for other files.",
        "Directory files work but must be added for each new service by hand, which is the maintenance the glob avoids.",
        "Rules read only the paths field. An unknown applies_to key is ignored, so the rule would load in every session.",
        "A root section loads in every session and depends on Claude inferring when it applies, rather than matching files."]],
    ["arch-public-specific", "3.3", "memory", ".claude/rules/migrations.md has `paths: [\"**/*.ts\"]`. Session logs show its rollback-note requirement loading while Claude edits apps/web/lib/query.ts, and Claude added rollback notes to 6 UI pull requests last week. Migrations live under services/*/db/migrations/ as .ts files. Which change fixes this with the smallest effect on the repository?",
      "Narrow the paths glob to services/*/db/migrations/**/*.ts, then confirm it loads for a migration but not for a UI file.",
      "When a path-scoped rule fires on the wrong files, fix the glob that matched them, then test one matching and one non-matching file. Counter-instructions elsewhere leave conflicting guidance loaded, moving it to user scope removes it from the team, and downstream checks clean up symptoms rather than the scope error.",
      "Keep the current glob and add 'disregard the migration rule in apps/web' to the root CLAUDE.md for UI sessions.",
      "Move the rule into your ~/.claude/rules/ directory so it applies in your own sessions rather than the team's.",
      "Add a CI check that rejects pull requests under apps/web whose description contains the phrase 'rollback note' anywhere.",
      ["**/*.ts matches every TypeScript file. Narrowing it to the migration directories fixes the cause, and the two-file test confirms it.",
        "Both instructions would still load for UI files, leaving Claude to resolve a contradiction on each edit.",
        "User-level rules stop the noise for you but remove the requirement from teammates who edit real migrations.",
        "A CI rejection detects the symptom after Claude has written the notes, while the overbroad glob keeps loading the rule."]],
    ["arch-public-plans", "3.4", "cli", "You must change the response shape of adapters/index, which services/search, apps/web and a reporting job consume. The change touches about 40 files, and two migration designs are viable. You want Claude to explore dependencies and propose an approach, with no edits until you approve. How should you start Claude Code?",
      "Launch with claude --permission-mode plan, or press Shift+Tab into plan mode, and approve its plan before edits begin.",
      "Plan mode is a permission mode where Claude reads, explores and proposes but cannot edit until you approve. Enter it with --permission-mode plan, Shift+Tab or /plan. Use it when the scenario already states multi-file, multi-consumer impact or competing designs; waiting for complexity to appear, or prescribing an unexplored design, invites rework.",
      "Start in the default mode with detailed upfront instructions documenting exactly how each consumer should change.",
      "Launch with claude --dry-run so Claude simulates each edit and prints a proposed diff without writing to the repository.",
      "Start in the default mode and switch to plan mode if Claude encounters unexpected dependencies partway through.",
      ["Plan mode blocks edits until approval and fits a 40-file change with three consumers and two competing designs.",
        "Detailed instructions assume you already know the right design, but the consumers' dependencies have not been explored yet.",
        "Claude Code has no --dry-run flag. Exploration and a proposal without edits is exactly what plan mode provides.",
        "The complexity is already stated in the task. Starting with edits risks rework across 40 files before the design is agreed."]],
    ["arch-public-small", "3.4", "cli", "A teammate reports the label 'Serach results' on one page, apps/web/pages/search.tsx line 88. A search confirms the string appears in that one place and nothing else reads it. The team's guidance says plan mode is for multi-file or architectural changes. Which workflow is proportionate?",
      "Use direct execution to fix line 88, then run that page's existing test to check the label renders correctly.",
      "Match the mode to stated scope and risk. A single known string with a confirmed location is a direct-execution change followed by a quick check. Planning or broad exploration adds a review cycle without reducing risk, and unscoped replacements widen the change beyond what was verified.",
      "Enter plan mode so Claude explores apps/web for related labels and proposes a reviewed fix before editing anything.",
      "Ask the Explore subagent to map the repository's strings first, then edit the label once it reports back.",
      "Run a repository-wide find-and-replace for 'Serach' across all packages so similar typos are fixed in one pass.",
      ["The location is known, nothing else depends on the string, and a quick test verifies the result.",
        "Planning a one-line, single-file correction adds an approval cycle without reducing any real risk to the codebase.",
        "Mapping every string in the repository spends context and time on discovery the teammate has already done.",
        "An unscoped replacement may change identifiers or fixtures the search never examined, widening a verified one-line fix."]],
    ["arch-public-test", "3.5", "eval", "After Claude changes normalizeQuery, CI reports `expected \"café latte\" but received \"cafe latte\"` at query.test.ts line 31. Your last two messages to Claude said 'tests still fail, please fix', and each attempt edited different files. What feedback is most effective now?",
      "Paste the failing assertion, its input, the expected 'café latte' output, and state that accents must be kept.",
      "Concrete failures beat restated intent. Give Claude the exact assertion, input and expected output so the next iteration targets the observed behavior. After two vague corrections, change the input rather than the emphasis. Broad cleanup requests widen the change, and editing the test to match the bug hides the regression.",
      "Ask Claude to review the whole search package for quality issues and then rerun the full test suite.",
      "Ask Claude to update the assertion to expect 'cafe latte' so the test matches normalizeQuery's current output.",
      "Restate the original feature request in stronger words and ask Claude to think harder before it edits files.",
      ["The exact failure and expected output tell Claude what to change, ending the pattern of edits to unrelated files.",
        "A package-wide review widens the scope again, which is how the previous attempts drifted into other files.",
        "Changing the expectation hides a real regression: accents are now being stripped from the user's search queries.",
        "Stronger wording repeats the vague feedback that already failed twice, without new information about the failure."]],
    ["arch-public-criteria", "3.5", "prompt", "Claude's patch to the legacy adapter passes all 42 fixture tests, but production logs show 120 errors an hour on records where middle_name is an empty string, which old clients send instead of null. You will ask Claude for another iteration. Which instruction gives it the best target?",
      "Add a failing test where middle_name is \"\" and expect the same output as null, then iterate until it passes.",
      "When an edge case escapes, encode it as a specific test with input and expected output, then iterate until it passes. A test is an objective target and guards against regressions. Vague 'be robust' wording invites guessing, approving defers a known production defect, and swallowing errors hides it.",
      "Approve the patch because the 42 fixtures pass, and open a separate refactor ticket for legacy input handling.",
      "Ask Claude to handle edge cases in middle names more robustly, then review the revised patch against the fixtures.",
      "Ask Claude to add try/catch logging around the adapter so empty-string errors are recorded without failing requests.",
      ["A concrete failing case with an expected result gives Claude an unambiguous target and keeps the fix from regressing.",
        "The fixtures miss the production case. Approving ships a known defect producing 120 errors an hour.",
        "'More robustly' does not say which input fails or what output is correct, so Claude must guess again.",
        "Logging and swallowing the error hides the defect and returns wrong results instead of fixing the conversion."]],
    ["arch-public-ci", "3.6", "cli", "The CI review step runs `claude \"Review pr.diff against CLAUDE.md\"`. The job hangs until its 30-minute timeout, and when a run does finish, a script scrapes the Markdown output and misses about 10% of findings. The review must not modify the workspace. Which TWO changes fix the step?",
      ["Run with -p plus --output-format json and --json-schema, then read findings from the structured_output field.", "Pre-approve only Read and Bash(git diff *) with --allowedTools, and bound the run with --max-turns."],
      "In CI, -p runs Claude Code non-interactively, --output-format json with --json-schema returns validated findings in structured_output, and --allowedTools plus --max-turns limit what the job can do. Invented environment variables and stdin tricks do not change Claude Code's mode, and scraping text output stays fragile.",
      "Set CLAUDE_HEADLESS=true in the job environment so Claude Code skips interactive prompts during the review.",
      "Redirect stdin from /dev/null and keep scraping the Markdown output with a stricter regular expression.",
      "Grant Edit and Write through --allowedTools so the review can apply its own suggested fixes directly in CI.",
      ["-p stops the hang, and schema-validated structured_output replaces the scraper that loses 10% of findings.",
        "A narrow tool list keeps the job read-only, and a turn bound stops runaway reviews from consuming the timeout.",
        "CLAUDE_HEADLESS is not a Claude Code setting. Non-interactive mode comes from the -p (or --print) flag.",
        "Redirecting stdin does not put Claude Code into print mode, and a stricter regex is still text scraping.",
        "Edit and Write let the job change the workspace, which the requirements forbid for this review step."]],
    ["arch-public-ci-error", "3.6", "cli", "The review job runs `claude -p` with --output-format json, --json-schema and --max-turns 10. Today it reached the turn limit: the result's subtype was error_max_turns and it had no structured_output, so a wrapper wrote {\"findings\": []} as the report. Policy requires a completed review of the current diff before merge. What should the gate do?",
      "Fail the gate when structured_output is absent, mark the review incomplete, and rerun it under the retry policy.",
      "A gate must check that the run completed and produced validated output, not just that a file parses. With --json-schema, a valid answer appears in structured_output; when that field is missing, treat the review as incomplete. Empty arrays, older commits' reviews and warnings do not satisfy a completed-review requirement.",
      "Pass the gate because the wrapper's report is schema-valid and contains no findings that would block merging.",
      "Reuse the last completed review from the parent commit and schedule a fresh review once the merge is done.",
      "Pass the gate with a warning annotation and page the on-call engineer to review the diff manually later that day.",
      ["The missing structured_output and error_max_turns subtype show the review never finished, so the policy is not met.",
        "The empty array came from the wrapper, not from a completed review. A schema-valid file does not prove the review ran.",
        "The parent commit's review covers different code, and reviewing after merge defeats a pre-merge gate.",
        "A warning still lets unreviewed code merge, which is exactly what the completed-review policy is meant to forbid."]],
    ["arch-public-discovery", "3.2", "skills", "The /api-review skill's SKILL.md is 2,400 lines, including 180 request and response examples. /context shows it consuming about 35,000 tokens each time it runs, although 90% of runs need only the 12-step checklist at the top. How should the skill be repackaged?",
      "Keep the checklist in SKILL.md and move the examples to a file in the skill folder that it references when needed.",
      "Skills support progressive disclosure: a lean SKILL.md loads on invocation, and bundled reference files load only when the task needs them. Instructions to disregard content do not stop it entering context, CLAUDE.md loads in every session, and forking moves the cost into a subagent without removing it.",
      "Move the examples into the root CLAUDE.md so they are already loaded when the skill starts running.",
      "Add context: fork to the frontmatter so the examples load inside a subagent instead of the main session.",
      "Add a line at the top of SKILL.md telling Claude to disregard the examples unless a request is unusual.",
      ["Most runs then load the 12-step checklist alone, and the examples are read for the 10% of runs that need them.",
        "CLAUDE.md loads in every session, so the examples would cost tokens even when the skill never runs.",
        "A forked subagent still loads the full skill body on each run; the 35,000 tokens move rather than disappear.",
        "The whole SKILL.md body enters context on invocation, so an instruction to disregard part of it saves nothing."]],
  ]),
  ...retestCase("Structured Data Extraction", "You are building a structured data extraction system for a grants foundation. Claude extracts award amounts, payment schedules, dates and contacts from signed grant agreements and their attachments by calling an extract_grant tool whose input_schema defines the record. Outputs are validated and imported into a finance system. Conflicting or weakly supported records go to staff reviewers, and historical backfills run in bulk.", [
    ["arch-canopy-rubric", "4.1", "eval", "An automated reviewer grades each extracted effective_date. Its prompt says 'flag dates that look wrong; be conservative.' On a 200-record audit it flagged 61 dates, staff agreed with 19 of them, and two runs over the same records disagreed on 37 verdicts. Which change most improves precision and consistency?",
      "Define which clause controls the effective date and when to flag, with labeled examples of correct, wrong and ambiguous dates.",
      "Precision comes from categorical criteria the reviewer can apply: what to report, what to skip and what each case looks like, anchored by examples. 'Be conservative' and 'high confidence' give no rule to apply, a bigger model reasons over the same vague criteria, and majority voting suppresses real but intermittent catches.",
      "Tell the reviewer to report high-confidence problems alone and to explain its reasoning before each verdict.",
      "Move the reviewer to a larger model with extended thinking so it judges each date more carefully.",
      "Run the reviewer three times per record and flag a date when at least two of the runs agree.",
      ["A stated rule plus examples gives every run the same decision boundary, which addresses both the 42 false flags and the 37 inconsistent verdicts.",
        "'High confidence' is another mood rather than a rule, and the model's sense of confidence is not calibrated to what staff consider wrong.",
        "A larger model still has to interpret 'look wrong', so the vague criterion that causes inconsistency remains.",
        "Voting smooths run-to-run noise but keeps the same unclear criterion and can suppress correct flags that appear intermittently."]],
    ["arch-canopy-evidence", "4.1", "prompt", "A check must flag award amounts that the agreement does not support. Last month it returned 'amount looks fine' for 412 of 420 records, yet staff later found 23 amounts copied from budget drafts rather than from the signed award clause. What should the check be required to return for each amount?",
      "The amount, the clause and page that state it, and a status of supported, unsupported or not stated.",
      "Make review outputs observable: require the evidence location for each value and an explicit unsupported or not-stated outcome. Then an auditor can check why something passed. Free-text verdicts hide the basis, uncalibrated confidence does not prove support, and statistical outliers test plausibility rather than whether the signed clause says it.",
      "A pass or fail verdict with a one-sentence justification written for the finance team's weekly summary.",
      "The amount plus a confidence score from 0 to 1, with scores below 0.6 routed to staff for review.",
      "The amount compared with the median award for similar grants, flagging values more than 30% away from it.",
      ["Requiring the clause and page makes a budget-draft source visible immediately, and 'unsupported' becomes a reportable outcome.",
        "A one-line justification is how 'looks fine' slipped through; it does not show which document the amount came from.",
        "Self-reported confidence was presumably high on the 23 copied amounts, so a threshold would still pass them.",
        "Budget-draft amounts are often close to the award, so an outlier test checks plausibility rather than support."]],
    ["arch-canopy-examples", "4.2", "prompt", "The prompt already says 'use the date the agreement was signed, not proposed dates.' Still, 14% of extractions take the 'Proposed start date' from page 1 instead of the countersignature date on the last page. The prompt's 3 examples are all agreements that contain only one date. Which change to the examples best fixes this?",
      "Use paired agreements that contain both dates, showing why the signature date wins over the proposed one.",
      "When instructions are clear but output is still wrong, add examples that sit on the ambiguous boundary and show the reasoning. Pairs that differ in the controlling feature teach the distinction; more easy positives, louder wording or layout variety leave the confusing case unshown, so the model keeps matching surface cues.",
      "Add five more single-date signed agreements so the model sees more correct signature-date extractions.",
      "Rewrite the instruction in capitals and repeat it at the end of the prompt, after the existing examples.",
      "Add examples from three new templates with different layouts but still one date per agreement.",
      ["Examples containing both dates show the exact confusion and the reasoning for the choice, which the single-date examples never do.",
        "More easy positives never show a proposed date being rejected, so the 14% error pattern is untouched.",
        "The instruction is already clear; louder repetition is still a description rather than a demonstration.",
        "Layout variety helps generalization but still never presents the two-date case that causes the errors."]],
    ["arch-canopy-edge", "4.2", "prompt", "The extractor treats every signed attachment containing a dollar amount as a replacement payment schedule. In a 300-document sample, 31 allocation notes, which only split an award across budget lines, were imported as new schedules. Policy says only an explicit replacement clause changes payment dates. You have room for two examples. Which pair teaches the distinction?",
      "Two signed attachments with similar amounts, one with a replacement clause and one allocation note, each labeled with its outcome.",
      "The most informative examples hold surface features constant and vary only the controlling detail. Here signature and amount are shared, and the replacement clause decides the outcome. Varied positives, signature-status pairs or negatives alone never show the model which clause actually changes the schedule.",
      "Two signed amendments that each replace the schedule, with different amounts and payees to show variety.",
      "A signed amendment and an unsigned draft with the same replacement clause, each labeled with its outcome.",
      "Two allocation notes from different templates, each labeled 'no change', to show what non-replacements look like.",
      ["Holding signature and amount constant isolates the replacement clause, which is the feature the 31 errors ignored.",
        "Both examples are positives, so the model never sees a signed attachment with an amount that leaves dates unchanged.",
        "This teaches the signature boundary, but the errors involve signed allocation notes, not unsigned drafts.",
        "Negatives alone do not show what a real replacement looks like, so the contrast the model needs is still missing."]],
    ["arch-canopy-schema", "4.3", "structured", "Requests send the extract_grant tool with tool_choice {\"type\": \"auto\"}. In 3% of 10,000 calls Claude replies with prose such as 'Here is the extracted data:' and no tool call, and the importer crashes. Each request should produce exactly one extract_grant record that matches the schema. Which request change fixes this?",
      "Set tool_choice to {\"type\": \"tool\", \"name\": \"extract_grant\"} and add strict: true to the tool definition.",
      "A forced tool choice makes Claude respond with a tool_use block for that tool, and strict: true guarantees the input conforms to input_schema. With auto, text replies remain possible. Regex cleanup and prompt rules lower the failure rate without removing it, and 'required' is not a tool_choice type in the Claude API.",
      "Keep auto and strip leading prose with a regular expression before parsing the first JSON object in the reply.",
      "Set tool_choice to {\"type\": \"required\"} so Claude must return the extract_grant schema instead of text.",
      "Keep auto and add a system-prompt line stating that replies must contain only the extract_grant tool call.",
      ["Forcing the named tool removes prose replies, and strict mode guarantees the tool input conforms to input_schema.",
        "Prose replies may contain no JSON at all, and regex extraction does not guarantee types or required fields.",
        "tool_choice accepts auto, any, tool and none. 'required' is not a valid type and the request would be rejected.",
        "A prompt line lowers the 3% failure rate but cannot guarantee a tool call the way a forced tool_choice can."]],
    ["arch-canopy-missing", "4.3", "structured", "matching_funds is required and typed number in the schema. About 40% of agreements never mention matching funds, and finance found 1,100 records showing 0 that, on review, required matching funds stated in an attachment that was not provided. Which schema change preserves the difference between absent and zero?",
      "Make matching_funds nullable as [\"number\", \"null\"], keep it required, and define null as 'not stated'.",
      "Required non-nullable fields force a value even when the source is silent, which turns absence into fabricated zeros. Make such fields nullable, say what null means, and keep them required so omission remains a validation error. Sentinel numbers and free-text notes shift interpretation onto every downstream consumer.",
      "Make matching_funds optional, so the field is simply omitted whenever the agreement does not mention it.",
      "Keep it a required number and instruct Claude to use -1 when the agreement does not state an amount.",
      "Keep it a required number and add a matching_funds_note string field for Claude to explain zero values.",
      ["Null now means 'not stated', zero means a stated zero, and requiring the key still catches records where the field was dropped.",
        "An optional field makes an accidental omission look identical to a legitimate 'not stated', so errors go undetected.",
        "-1 is a sentinel that every consumer must know about; finance totals would silently include negative amounts.",
        "A free-text note leaves the numeric field as a misleading zero and makes consumers parse prose to interpret it."]],
    ["arch-canopy-crossfield", "4.4", "structured", "A schema-valid record shows an award of $120,000 with instalments of $40,000, $40,000 and $30,000. The schedule on page 4 lists a third instalment of $40,000. Validation currently checks types only, and 2% of last month's imports had similar gaps. What should the pipeline do?",
      "Check that instalments sum to the award, then retry with the document, the failed output and the exact mismatch.",
      "Schemas guarantee shape, not arithmetic or truth. Add semantic validation, and when it fails and the information exists in the source, retry with the document, the failed output and the specific error. Blind retries repeat the mistake, silent code corrections can hide genuine document conflicts, and nightly reports find bad data after import.",
      "Resubmit the same request unchanged, since strict schema validation means a second attempt usually corrects totals.",
      "Adjust the last instalment to $40,000 in code so that the instalments match the award before importing.",
      "Import the record and add a nightly report listing awards whose instalments do not sum to the total.",
      ["A sum check catches the $10,000 gap, and a retry naming the mismatch lets Claude re-read page 4 and correct the instalment.",
        "The schema only guarantees types. An unchanged retry has no information about the error and may repeat it.",
        "Code cannot know which instalment is wrong, and forcing a match would hide documents whose totals genuinely conflict.",
        "A nightly report finds bad records after finance has already imported them, which is the outcome to prevent."]],
    ["arch-canopy-targetrepair", "4.4", "grounding", "Validation finds that final_report_due (2026-06-30) came from a reminder notice, while the signed addendum on page 7 sets 2026-09-30. The other 17 fields passed every check. A full re-extraction costs about 12,000 tokens and changed previously valid fields in 8% of past retries. What should the repair request contain?",
      "The failed field, both dates with their pages, and the addendum passage, asking Claude to fix that one field.",
      "Targeted repair preserves verified work. Send the specific failed field, the conflict and the authoritative passage so Claude can fix that value with evidence. Full re-extractions cost more and can regress valid fields, while bare 'it's wrong' feedback gives the model nothing concrete to act on.",
      "The full agreement with an instruction to re-extract every field more carefully, using a larger max_tokens budget.",
      "The record and a note saying 'final_report_due is wrong, please fix it', without any of the source passages.",
      "The full agreement plus last week's five corrected records as examples, asking Claude to repeat the extraction.",
      ["The request carries the evidence for the one failed field and leaves the 17 verified fields untouched.",
        "Re-extracting everything costs 12,000 tokens and risks the 8% regression rate on fields that already passed.",
        "Without the addendum passage Claude has no evidence for the correct date and may simply pick the other value.",
        "Generic examples do not address this conflict, and a full re-extraction still puts the valid fields at risk."]],
    ["arch-canopy-batch", "4.5", "batch", "The foundation must backfill 18,000 historical agreements by Monday morning, three days away. Each extraction is one forced extract_grant call with no follow-up tool use. Separately, staff use a live intake form that must return an extraction within 20 seconds. Which processing design fits both workloads?",
      "Send the backfill through Message Batches with a stable custom_id per agreement, and keep intake on synchronous calls.",
      "Message Batches suits latency-tolerant, single-shot bulk work: half price, results within 24 hours, matched by custom_id. Interactive paths with seconds-level deadlines stay synchronous. Moving everything to batches breaks the live SLA, and multi-document prompts blur attribution and make per-record retries harder.",
      "Move both the backfill and the live intake to Message Batches so each extraction gets the 50% batch discount.",
      "Run the backfill as synchronous calls at 50 concurrent requests so it completes sooner than a batch could.",
      "Combine agreements into prompts of 50 documents each and ask for a list of records per synchronous call.",
      ["The backfill tolerates hours of delay and is single-shot, so batching halves its cost; the 20-second intake stays synchronous.",
        "Batches carry no latency guarantee and can take up to 24 hours, which breaks the 20-second intake requirement.",
        "Synchronous processing works but pays full price for 18,000 extractions that have three days of slack.",
        "Multi-document prompts mix 50 records in one response, making attribution and per-agreement retries much harder."]],
    ["arch-canopy-batch-retry", "4.5", "batch", "A batch of 5,000 extractions ended with 4,962 succeeded, 31 errored and 7 expired results, and the importer has already stored the successes. The current script joins each result to its source file by the result's position in the results file. Which recovery is most appropriate?",
      "Join by custom_id, inspect the 38 failures, fix those that need changes, and resubmit only them under their IDs.",
      "Batch results can arrive in any order, so join on custom_id, never on position. Recover by resubmitting only errored or expired requests, after fixing whatever caused them, under IDs that still map to their sources. Rerunning everything wastes cost and risks duplicates, and batches have no built-in retry-failures option.",
      "Resubmit the full 5,000-request batch with the same IDs and let the importer deduplicate the stored records.",
      "Keep the positional join, since results return in submission order, and resubmit the 38 failed positions as a new batch.",
      "Call the batch retry endpoint with only_failed: true so the API reprocesses the 38 failures in place.",
      ["custom_id is the reliable join key, and resubmitting the 38 failures alone preserves the 4,962 stored successes.",
        "Rerunning 4,962 successful requests roughly doubles cost and relies on deduplication to prevent duplicate imports.",
        "Results are not guaranteed to follow submission order, so the positional join may already have attached wrong records.",
        "The Message Batches API has no retry endpoint or only_failed option; failed requests are resubmitted in a new batch."]],
    ["arch-canopy-blind", "4.6", "eval", "A second-pass reviewer receives the extractor's full conversation, including its reasoning, and is asked to check the record. Across 500 audited records it agreed with the extractor 98% of the time, yet staff found errors in 41 of the records it approved. What change most improves the review?",
      "Use a fresh instance given the agreement, the record and the criteria, without the extractor's reasoning.",
      "A reviewer that sees the generator's reasoning tends to re-derive and approve the same conclusions. Independence comes from a fresh context that holds only the artifact, the source and the criteria. Instructions to be critical or more thinking budget operate on the same shared premises, and self-review shares every blind spot.",
      "Keep the shared conversation but instruct the reviewer to challenge each of the extractor's conclusions critically.",
      "Keep the shared conversation and give the reviewer extended thinking with a larger budget for each record.",
      "Have the extractor review its own record a second time in the same session before handing it off.",
      ["A fresh context removes the extractor's rationalizations, so the reviewer reads the source as a stranger would.",
        "The reviewer still starts from the extractor's reasoning, so an instruction to be critical does not remove the anchor.",
        "More thinking over the same premises tends to re-derive the same conclusions, explaining the 98% agreement.",
        "Self-review in the same session shares every assumption that produced the 41 errors in the first place."]],
    ["arch-canopy-judge", "4.6", "eval", "Two independent review instances disagree on whether attachment B changes a grant's payment schedule. One cites a signed amendment dated 3 March; the other cites an unsigned draft dated 9 March. This pattern occurred in 26 of 900 records last quarter. How should the pipeline decide such cases?",
      "Apply the precedence rule, signed over unsigned, and send cases it cannot settle to a staff member.",
      "Disagreement between independent reviewers is a signal to apply an explicit evidence rule, then escalate what the rule cannot decide. Self-rated confidence and recency do not establish authority, and majority voting among similar instances can repeat the same mistake rather than resolve which source governs.",
      "Use the review from the instance reporting higher self-rated confidence, and log both citations for audit.",
      "Prefer the more recent document, because the 9 March draft reflects the latest negotiated terms.",
      "Run a third independent instance and accept the schedule that two of the three instances choose.",
      ["The precedence rule settles these cases from evidence, and escalation covers the ones the rule does not decide.",
        "Self-rated confidence does not show which document governs; logging both citations does not resolve the dispute.",
        "An unsigned draft has no authority to change terms, whatever its date, so recency picks the wrong source here.",
        "A tie-breaking vote among similar instances can repeat the same misreading instead of applying the governing rule."]],
    ["arch-canopy-facts", "5.1", "context", "A 6-hour review of grant G-2207 is about to be compacted. Earlier the agent verified a replacement due date of 2026-09-30 from addendum revision 3, page 7, and four of nine checks remain. Past compactions turned exact dates into phrases like 'late September'. Which TWO pieces of state should be kept outside the summary?",
      ["A case-facts block holding the verified due date 2026-09-30 and the addendum revision 3 it came from.", "The page 7 evidence reference and the list of the four checks that still need to run."],
      "Compaction paraphrases, so exact amounts, dates, IDs and evidence pointers belong in a persistent case-facts block sent with every request, alongside what remains to be done. Superseded values add confusion, raw document dumps defeat the purpose of compaction, and a model-written narrative is exactly where precision gets lost.",
      "The first draft due date from the original agreement, since it was the first value the agent recorded.",
      "The full raw text of each document read so far, appended to every request after the compaction.",
      "A narrative summary written by Claude that describes the review's progress in its own words.",
      ["A structured block keeps the exact date and its revision intact, which compaction previously blurred into 'late September'.",
        "The evidence pointer lets later steps recheck the value, and the remaining checks stop work being skipped or repeated.",
        "The original date was superseded by the addendum; preserving it alone would restore the wrong value.",
        "Re-sending six hours of raw documents defeats compaction and buries the verified facts in unneeded text.",
        "A model-written narrative is the summary that already lost precision; it is not a substitute for structured facts."]],
    ["arch-canopy-conflict", "5.2", "grounding", "A signed agreement names Dana Ruiz as grantee contact, while a later, unsigned cover letter names Lee Park. Neither the foundation's precedence policy nor the agreement says which governs contact changes, and the contact receives payment notices. Nine such records arrived this month. What should the extraction pipeline do?",
      "Keep both contacts with where each came from, mark the field as conflicting, and route it to staff.",
      "When credible sources conflict and no rule settles which governs, keep both values with attribution and escalate to someone with authority. Picking by recency or by a generalized rule the policy does not state hides the uncertainty, and nulling a consequential field silently disrupts downstream work.",
      "Choose the later cover letter's contact and mark the field provisional until a reviewer raises an objection.",
      "Choose the signed agreement's contact, since signed documents outrank unsigned ones for each extracted field.",
      "Leave the contact field null and import the record, so payment notices pause until someone notices the gap.",
      ["Both values stay attributed, the conflict is visible, and a person with authority decides who receives payment notices.",
        "Recency does not establish authority, and a provisional value still sends payment notices to a possibly wrong person.",
        "The policy is silent on contact changes, so applying a general signed-first rule makes a decision it never authorized.",
        "A null field disrupts payment notices silently and loses both candidate values the reviewer would need."]],
    ["arch-canopy-errors", "5.3", "subagents", "An extraction subagent read grant G-3110's main agreement, then timed out after 120 seconds fetching addendum A. It currently returns {\"status\": \"error\", \"message\": \"timeout\"}, and the coordinator re-runs the entire extraction, which has timed out the same way on 4 of the last 10 attempts. What should the subagent return?",
      "The verified main-agreement fields, the addendum fetch's failure type and request, and a note that addendum terms are unverified.",
      "Subagents should return structured failure context: what succeeded, what failed and how, and what remains unverified. The coordinator can then retry only the missing piece or route the gap. Marking partial work as success hides risk, generic errors force blind re-runs, and stopping the whole run wastes valid work.",
      "A complete record built from the main agreement, marked successful, since addenda change terms in few grants.",
      "Retry internally with backoff and return a generic 'extraction unavailable' message once retries run out.",
      "Raise the timeout to the coordinator's top-level handler so the whole backfill run stops for investigation.",
      ["Structured context lets the coordinator keep the verified fields and retry just the addendum fetch instead of the full extraction.",
        "Marking the record successful hides that addendum terms, which can change payment dates, were never checked.",
        "A generic message after retries still tells the coordinator nothing about what was verified or what failed.",
        "Stopping the entire backfill for one slow fetch discards valid work across every other grant."]],
    ["arch-canopy-investigation", "5.4", "context", "An engineer is investigating why extraction accuracy fell from 96% to 88% after a grant template change. After 3 hours the session has read 60 files across the parser, prompts and validators, and Claude's answers now contradict findings from the first hour. The work continues in a fresh session tomorrow. What handoff is most effective?",
      "Write a scratchpad file listing established causes with file paths, ruled-out hypotheses and open questions, then start from it.",
      "Long investigations degrade as context fills with stale and verbose tool output. Persist findings outside the conversation, including what was ruled out and what remains open, and start the next phase from that summary. Resuming carries the degraded context forward, bigger windows delay the problem, and re-reading everything repeats the cost.",
      "Resume the same session tomorrow so all 60 file reads remain available without repeating any of the investigation.",
      "Switch to a model with a larger context window so the whole investigation fits in one session without degrading.",
      "Start fresh tomorrow and let Claude re-read all 60 files, so its findings reflect a clean and complete context.",
      ["A persistent record of verified findings and ruled-out hypotheses lets tomorrow's session start focused instead of rediscovering them.",
        "The contradictions show the context has already degraded; resuming carries those 60 verbose reads forward.",
        "A larger window delays degradation but keeps stale early reads in context, competing with the later findings.",
        "Re-reading all 60 files repeats three hours of exploration and loses the hypotheses that were already ruled out."]],
    ["arch-canopy-review-risk", "5.5", "eval", "Staff can review 150 of the 2,000 records extracted each day. Errors in award amounts over $250,000 cost far more than errors in contact names, and 6% of records carry a conflict flag or missing evidence. The current policy reviews a uniform random 150. Which routing policy is most appropriate?",
      "Send high-value, conflicted or weakly supported records to staff first, and sample the rest by stratum.",
      "Allocate scarce review by consequence and evidence quality, and keep sampling what is automated so drift is still caught. Uniform samples spend capacity on low-risk records, length is a weak proxy for risk, and uncalibrated self-confidence leaves high-confidence errors entirely unchecked.",
      "Keep the uniform random 150, because it gives an unbiased estimate of the pipeline's overall accuracy.",
      "Review the 150 longest extractions, since longer agreements contain more fields that could be wrong.",
      "Review records where Claude's self-reported confidence is below 0.7 and accept the rest without sampling.",
      ["Capacity goes where errors cost most and evidence is weakest, while stratified sampling keeps measuring the automated records.",
        "A uniform sample measures accuracy but spends most of the 150 reviews on low-stakes records.",
        "Length is a weak proxy; a short agreement with a $400,000 award matters more than a long one with small amounts.",
        "Self-reported confidence is not calibrated, and dropping sampling leaves high-confidence errors on accepted records unseen."]],
    ["arch-canopy-calibrate", "5.5", "eval", "Records scoring above 0.9 confidence skip review. An audit found that 9 of 60 high-confidence amounts from scanned agreements were unsupported, compared with 1 of 400 from digital PDFs, and overall accuracy is 97%. What should the team measure before deciding when this score should route records to staff?",
      "Chart confidence bands against audited correctness, split by scan versus digital PDF and by field.",
      "Aggregate accuracy can hide failures in one slice. Calibrate confidence against labeled outcomes by document type and field before letting it route work. Arbitrary threshold changes ignore whether the score predicts correctness, thresholds from one slice may not transfer, and agreement between similar instances is not verification.",
      "Raise the global threshold to 0.95 until the number of auto-accepted records falls by about a third.",
      "Derive the threshold from digital PDFs, where the score performs well, and apply it across document types.",
      "Ask a second instance of the same model for its confidence and auto-accept records where both scores agree.",
      ["Per-slice calibration shows whether the score predicts correctness for scans, where 15% of high-confidence amounts failed.",
        "Cutting volume does not show that the remaining high scores are reliable, especially on scanned agreements.",
        "The score behaves well on digital PDFs; a threshold learned there may not transfer to scans at all.",
        "Two copies of one model can share the same blind spot, so their agreement is not independent verification."]],
    ["arch-canopy-provenance", "5.6", "grounding", "The finance system shows grant G-1804's due date as 2026-09-30. An auditor asks which document supports it, but the record stores only source: 'grant folder', and that folder holds 14 files, including two addenda with different dates. Which provenance design should the extraction pipeline adopt?",
      "For each field, store the document ID and revision, the page, and the exact passage that supports the value.",
      "Provenance should live at claim level: document, revision, location and excerpt for each value, preserved through every step. Folder links and execution metadata cannot show which source supports a field, bulk copies shift the search to the auditor, and after-the-fact explanations can invent a plausible but wrong source.",
      "Store a link to the grant folder plus the extraction timestamp and the reviewing analyst's username for each record.",
      "Store the full text of all 14 documents with the record so the auditor can search them for the date.",
      "Store a one-sentence explanation, written by Claude after extraction, of where each value came from.",
      ["Field-level provenance points the auditor straight to the exact addendum revision and passage behind 2026-09-30.",
        "A folder link with execution metadata leaves the auditor choosing between two addenda with different dates.",
        "Copying all 14 documents moves the search onto the auditor without showing which one was actually used.",
        "An explanation written after the fact can name a plausible source that was not the one actually used."]],
    ["arch-canopy-branches", "5.4", "sessions", "Two engineers will each test a different fix for the accuracy drop, starting from yesterday's investigation. The only handoff is a 300-word summary with no file paths, and both earlier retries spent about 50 file reads just relocating the parser. What should be added before they branch?",
      "A state manifest listing known file paths, each finding with its supporting excerpt, and the hypothesis per branch.",
      "Reproducible handoffs carry structured state: verified paths, findings tied to evidence and open hypotheses. That lets parallel branches start from the same baseline without re-exploring. Longer prose still lacks pointers, full transcripts reload the degraded context, and timing data does not tell anyone where the evidence is.",
      "A longer prose summary of about 1,500 words that describes the investigation's reasoning in more detail.",
      "The complete transcript of yesterday's session, pasted into the first message of each engineer's new session.",
      "A note of how long each investigation step took, so the engineers can judge which areas deserve more of their time.",
      ["Paths and evidence remove the 50-read relocation cost, and named hypotheses keep the two branches from overlapping.",
        "More prose still contains no file paths, so each engineer would again search for the parser.",
        "A full transcript reloads yesterday's degraded context, contradictions included, into both of the new sessions.",
        "Step durations describe effort rather than evidence and do not say where any finding lives."]],
    ["arch-canopy-critical", "5.1", "context", "Grant agreements contain bank account details that policy forbids sending to the model again after initial extraction. A 4-hour reconciliation session must continue after compaction, and the first compaction attempt dropped two verified amounts. Which state should the session carry forward?",
      "Source references, settled amounts and dates with their origins, and open conflicts, but no banking details.",
      "After compaction, carry compact, structured state: verified values with provenance, open conflicts and references to sources. That keeps precision without resending data policy excludes. Full documents violate the restriction and waste context, re-deriving loses verified work, and model-written summaries are where exact values were already lost.",
      "Each source document inline, banking pages included, so the session never needs to look anything up again.",
      "The user's original request alone, letting Claude re-derive verified amounts from document references as needed.",
      "Claude's own compacted summary, relying on it to keep amounts precise now that the first attempt was reviewed.",
      ["Structured verified facts and references restore the two dropped amounts' precision without resending restricted banking data.",
        "Resending banking pages breaks the stated policy and floods the compacted context with unneeded text.",
        "Re-deriving discards four hours of verified work and reopens amounts and dates that were already settled.",
        "The compacted summary is what dropped two verified amounts; reviewing one attempt does not make the next one reliable."]],
  ]),
];
